WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.300
We have a lot of choices
to make about our diet.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.180
Add to that, doing
the right thing

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.380
when it comes
to preventing

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.700
and treating a
chronic disease,

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.040
fighting a virus,
or losing weight,

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.440
and suddenly our
nutrition choices

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.840
can seem almost
overwhelming.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.240
Well, I’m here to help.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.460
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.560
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.820
More than 10,000 articles
have been published on gluten

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.940
in medical journals—
intimidating even for me!

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:31.860
Today we have a
three part series

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.680
summarizing the best
available science on gluten.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.280
In our first story, we look at
how we might prevent

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:42.420
the inflammation that occurs
from gluten-free diets.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:45.240
Over the last decade or so,
there’s been a growing interest

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.120
in the medical literature
on plant-based eating,

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:52.380
beyond just exclusionary
terms like vegetarian or vegan.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.380
Previous such studies
were somewhat limited

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.300
because they just split
people up into either

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:02.400
vegetarian or nonvegetarian,
excluding some or all animal foods,

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.580
but without taking into account
the quality of plant foods.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.720
Vegans could be living off of
French fries, Oreos, and Coca-Cola.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.880
Enter: “Changes in Plant-Based
Diet Quality and Total and

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.660
Cause-Specific Mortality,”
the first study to investigate

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.780
the associations between
changes in plant-based diet quality

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.520
and subsequent risk of death.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.640
If you look at overall
plant-based diet scores,

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.520
the more you increase your
intake of any plant foods and

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.320
decrease your intake
of any animal foods,

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.720
the lower your risk of
death appears to fall,

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.900
but that’s because people
were adding healthy plant foods

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:37.860
like fruits and vegetables.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.560
If you just look at people
who ate more processed,

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.520
sugary junk over time,
their mortality risk goes up,

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.000
the clinical implications
being that increasing intake

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.100
of healthy plant foods
and decreasing intake

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.100
of not just animal foods,
but also animal crackers,

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.980
could lower the future risk
of premature death.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:59.100
Whereas just reducing the
intake of carbs in general,

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.620
cutting down not just
refined carbs and sugars,

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.140
but healthy high carb foods
like fruits and whole grains

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:07.500
is associated with
increased overall mortality,

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:11.280
which would correspond to years
of your life being cut short,

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.560
as well as the risk
of dying specifically

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.080
from heart disease,
stroke, and cancer.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.860
Why are whole plant
foods so good for us?

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:23.580
Much of the benefit may be
mediated by our microbiome.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.880
There are all sorts of things
threatening our good gut bugs,

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.640
like the over prescription
of antibiotics, however,

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:33.420
the only factor that has been
empirically shown to be important

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.860
is a diet low in microbiome-
accessible carbohydrates,

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:41.760
in other words prebiotics
like fiber and resistant starch

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.660
only found one place in
abundance, whole plant foods.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:50.760
We went from high-MAC
diets to Big Mac diets

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.620
and may be suffering
the consequences.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.420
Intake of dietary fiber, which is
the main source of MACs in the diet

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:01.080
is negligibly low compared
to how we evolved.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:05.760
Such a low-fiber diet provides
insufficient food for our gut microbes,

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.060
starving ourselves of all the
wonderful things they produce for us.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:12.720
A low-fiber diet is a key
driver of microbiome depletion,

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.120
and this loss is implicated
in the rampant increase

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.420
of chronic diseases that
now plague the modern world.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.620
We evolved getting perhaps
a hundred grams of fiber a day.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:25.920
Our gut bugs must
have been in heaven.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.680
How do you get even close to that?

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.420
A cup of fruit may only have about

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:33.060
3 grams, a cup of
vegetables, 5 grams.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:36.180
Why so little? Because
fruits and vegetables

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:38.280
are like 80 to 90 percent water.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.760
One has to go to the drier plant
foods to really scale it up,

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.900
like beans, at 15 grams,
or intact grains like barley,

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.760
over 30 grams per cup.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.520
But many people are avoiding
common grains these days,

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.740
like wheat barley and
rye due to gluten,

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:56.340
but there is a dark side
of gluten-free diets.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.880
Now, if you have a condition
like celiac disease,

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:03.060
then you absolutely have to
avoid gluten, but if not,

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:06.660
the downsides include the potential
for nutritional deﬁciencies,

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:09.840
like not getting enough
fiber, and toxic compounds,

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.940
for example the
accumulation of heavy metals

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:14.100
in people on gluten-free diets.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.280
Those following a gluten-free
diet had signiﬁcantly increased

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:20.700
blood mercury levels, more
arsenic flowing through their systems.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:24.420
The arsenic is likely due to
increased rice consumption because

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.240
rice is a major ingredient
in gluten-free foods,

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.700
but you can certainly choose
other grains like sorghum—

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:31.620
that’s my favorite rice substitute.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.560
And the higher mercury may
just be because people eating

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.560
gluten-free diets were tending
to eat more fish for some reason.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:42.000
But the reason that 10 out of
10 of those without celiac disease

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.420
or a recognized gluten sensitivity
following a gluten-free diet

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:49.440
experienced a pro-inﬂammatory
gastrointestinal environment

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:53.100
is likely due to starving
your microbial self,

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:56.400
not getting enough prebiotics,
not getting enough fiber

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.260
because you’re avoiding
grains like wheat.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:01.200
So, whatever diet you
choose for whatever reason,

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.432
make sure you’re
getting enough fiber.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:08.220
In our next story, we look at how
the effects of gluten on our health

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.206
have been debated for
more than 30 years.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:15.120
In 1980, researchers in England
reported a series of women

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.360
who suffered from chronic diarrhea
that resolved on a gluten-free diet,

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:21.600
yet did not have evidence of celiac disease,&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.440
the autoimmune disorder associated&nbsp;
with gluten intolerance.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:31.860
The medical profession was skeptical
at the time, and even 30 years later,

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:37.140
so much so, that much like patients
who had irritable bowel syndrome,

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:42.840
patients claiming non-celiac gluten sensitivity
were commonly referred to psychiatrists

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:46.020
because they were believed to have
an underlying mental illness.

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:50.220
Psychological testing of such patients,
however, found no evidence

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:53.640
that they were suffering from some
kind of psychosomatic hysteria.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:06:00.720
The medical profession has a history of
dismissing diseases as all in people’s heads—

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:06.420
PTSD, ulcerative colitis, migraines,
ulcers, asthma, Parkinson’s, MS.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:10.800
Despite resistance from the prevailing
medical community at the time,

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.740
these health problems have&nbsp;
subsequently been confirmed&nbsp;

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:17.520
to be credible physiologically-based disorders

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:20.280
rather than psychologically-based confabulations.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:27.780
On the flip side, the Internet is rife with
unsubstantiated claims about gluten free diets,

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:33.960
which has spilled over into the popular press
to make gluten the diet villain du jour

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:39.000
with claims like ‘17 million
Americans are gluten sensitive.’

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:44.220
However, it must be remembered
that this is also ‘big business.’

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:50.640
When literally billions of dollars are
at stake, it’s hard to trust anybody,

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:54.180
so as always, best to stick to the science.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:07:00.180
What sort of evidence do we have for the existence
of a condition presumed to be so widespread?

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:02.220
Not much.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:06.780
The evidence base for such claims
was unfortunately very thin

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:09.120
because we didn’t have&nbsp;
randomized controlled trials

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:12.540
demonstrating that the entity even exists.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:19.080
The gold-standard for confirming non-celiac
gluten sensitivity requires a gluten-free diet,

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:23.340
followed by a double-blind, randomized,
placebo-controlled food challenge.

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:27.420
Like you give someone a muffin
and they’re not told

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:32.700
if it’s a gluten free muffin or gluten filled
muffin – to control for placebo effects –

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:33.720
and see what happens.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:38.040
The reason this is necessary is
because when you actually do this,

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:44.580
a number of quote-unquote “gluten-sensitive”
patients don’t react at all to disguised gluten,

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:49.020
and instead react to the gluten-free
placebo, so it truly was in their heads.

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:55.140
But we never had that kind of
level of evidence until 2011,

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:59.100
when a double-blind, randomized placebo-
controlled trial was published,

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:02.940
which tested to see if patients complaining
of irritable bowel type symptoms,

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:07.620
who claimed they felt better on a gluten free
diet – despite not having celiac disease –

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:11.100
actually could tell if they&nbsp;
were given gluten free bread&nbsp;

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:14.220
and muffins or the&nbsp;
gluten-containing bread and muffins.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:19.140
They started out gluten-free
and symptom-free for two weeks

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:21.600
and then they were challenged
with the bread and muffins.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:25.140
Here’s what happened to the 15
patients who got the placebo,

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:29.520
meaning they started out on a gluten-free
diet and continued on a gluten-free diet.

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:31.980
They got worse.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:36.060
Just the thought that they may be eating
something that was bad for them

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:37.740
made them feel all crampy and bloated.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:40.260
This is what’s called a nocebo effect.

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:44.580
The placebo effect is when you give someone
something useless and they feel better.

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:49.800
The nocebo effect is when you give someone
something harmless and they feel worse.

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:57.180
But the small group that got
the actual gluten felt worse still.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:03.060
So, they concluded, this non-celiac
gluten intolerance may actually exist.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:06.480
It was a small study, though,&nbsp;
and even though they claimed&nbsp;

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.820
the gluten-free bread and&nbsp;
muffins were indistinguishable,

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:12.300
maybe at some level the patients
could tell which is which.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:18.780
So in 2012, researchers in&nbsp;
Italy took 920 patients that&nbsp;

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:21.000
had been diagnosed with&nbsp;
non-celiac gluten sensitivity

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:24.480
and put them to the test&nbsp;
with a double-blinded wheat&nbsp;

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:27.120
challenge by giving them not bread and muffins,

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:30.660
but capsules filled with&nbsp;
wheat flour or filled with&nbsp;

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:34.980
placebo flour, kind of a placebo&nbsp;
powder – no flour at all.

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:40.560
And more than two-thirds&nbsp;
failed the test, like they&nbsp;

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:43.500
got worse on the placebo or better on the wheat.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:48.000
But of those that passed,&nbsp;
there was a clear benefit&nbsp;

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:55.440
to staying on the wheat-free diet, confirming the
existence of a non-celiac wheat sensitivity.

00:09:56.280 --> 00:10:01.860
Now note they said wheat sensitivity,
not gluten sensitivity.

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:06.660
Gluten itself may not be&nbsp;
causing gut symptoms at all.

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:11.760
See, most people with wheat sensitivity
have a variety of other food sensitivities.

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:15.180
Two thirds are sensitive to&nbsp;
cow’s milk protein as well,

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:18.780
then eggs were the most common culprit after that.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:25.140
So if you put people on a diet low in
common triggers of irritable bowel symptoms

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:28.860
and then challenge them with gluten,
there’s no effect.

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:34.980
Same increase in symptoms with high gluten,
low gluten, or no gluten, calling into question

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:39.360
the very existence of&nbsp;
non-celiac gluten sensitivity.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:43.740
Interestingly, despite&nbsp;
being informed that avoiding&nbsp;

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:47.100
gluten wasn’t apparently doing&nbsp;
a thing for their gut symptoms,

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:51.420
many participants opted to&nbsp;
continue following a gluten-free&nbsp;

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:54.480
diet as they just subjectively&nbsp;
described “feeling better,”

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:58.740
so the researchers wondered&nbsp;
if avoiding gluten might&nbsp;

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:01.740
be improving the mood of&nbsp;
those with wheat sensitivity,

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:05.100
and indeed, short-term exposure to gluten appeared&nbsp;

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:07.740
to induce feelings of&nbsp;
depression in these patients.

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:12.960
But whether non-celiac gluten&nbsp;
sensitivity is a disease of the mind&nbsp;

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:18.582
or the gut, it is no longer a&nbsp;
condition that can be dismissed.

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:22.620
Finally today, how common
is gluten sensitivity?

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:25.080
Are there benefits to gluten?

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:28.740
Why does the medical profession
explicitly advise against people

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:30.840
who might suspect they 
may be gluten intolerant

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:32.940
from just going on
a gluten-free diet?

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:35.549
Let’s find out.

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:40.200
Until only a few years ago, almost
the whole of the scientific world

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:44.100
maintained that the wheat protein gluten
would provoke negative effects

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:47.880
only in people with rare conditions such
as celiac disease or wheat allergies,

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:52.740
but by the early part of 2013,
it was largely becoming accepted

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:57.660
that some nonceliac patients could suffer
from gluten or wheat sensitivity.

00:11:57.660 --> 00:12:02.100
And indeed a consensus panel of
experts now officially recognizes

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:06.720
three gluten-related conditions, wheat allergy,
celiac disease, and gluten sensitivity.

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:11.340
So what percent of the population
should avoid wheat?

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:18.120
Although 1 in a thousand may have a wheat allergy,
nearly 1 in a 100 may have celiac disease,

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:26.640
and it appears to be on the rise, though there’s
still less than about 1 in 10,000 chance

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:29.760
Americans will get diagnosed
with celiac in a given year.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:33.480
How common is wheat sensitivity?

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:39.600
Our best estimate at this point is in that same
kind of general range, slightly higher than 1%,

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:45.540
but still that’s potentially millions of
people who may have been suffering for years

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:49.080
who could have been cured
by simple dietary means,

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:53.640
yet went unrecognized and unhelped
by the medical profession.

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:57.960
Although gluten sensitivity continues
to gain medical credibility,

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:01.260
we still don’t know how it works, or
how much gluten can be tolerated,

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:04.740
if it’s reversible or not and what
the long-term complications

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:06.900
might be of not sticking to the diet.

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:10.380
Considering the lack of knowledge,
maybe people with gluten sensitivity

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:14.700
should be trying to reintroduce gluten
back into their diet every year

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:16.620
to see if it’s still causing problems.

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:21.120
The reason health professionals don’t
want to see people on gluten-free diets

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:24.960
unless absolutely necessary is
that for the 98% of people

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:28.080
people that don’t have
gluten issues, whole grains—

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.360
including the gluten grains -
wheat, barley and rye—

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:34.680
are health promoting, linked
to the reduced risk of

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:38.760
coronary heart disease, cancer, diabetes,
obesity and other chronic diseases.

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:41.400
Just like, because some
people have a peanut allergy,

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:44.640
doesn’t mean that everyone
should avoid peanuts.

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:49.620
There is no evidence to suggest
that following a gluten-free diet

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:52.140
has any significant benefits
in the general population.

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:57.240
Indeed, there is some evidence to suggest that
a gluten-free diet may adversely affect

00:13:57.240 --> 00:14:01.260
gut health in those without celiac disease
or gluten sensitivity or allergy.

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:05.460
They’re talking about this study that
found that a month on a gluten-free diet

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:08.400
may hurt our gut flora
and immune function,

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:12.720
potentially setting those on gluten-free
diets up for an overgrowth

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:16.200
of harmful bacteria in
their intestines. Why?

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:20.160
Because, ironically, of the beneficial
effects of the very components

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:24.000
wheat sensitive people have problems
with—like the FODMAP fructans

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:26.700
that act as prebiotics and
feed our good bacteria,

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:30.120
or the gluten itself,
which may boost immune function.

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:33.540
Less than a week of added&nbsp;
gluten protein significantly&nbsp;

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:36.240
increases natural killer cell activity,

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:41.340
which could be expected to improve our body’s
ability to fight cancer and viral infections.

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:45.720
High gluten bread improves
triglyceride levels better

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:48.720
than regular gluten bread,
as another example.

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:56.640
Ironically, one of the greatest threats of
gluten-free diets may be the gluten itself.

00:14:57.240 --> 00:15:02.640
Self-prescription of a gluten-free diet may
undermine our ability to pick up celiac disease,

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:04.920
the much more serious form
of gluten intolerance.

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:08.160
The way we diagnose celiac is
by looking for the inflammation

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:10.260
caused by gluten in
celiac sufferers,

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:13.620
but if they haven’t been eating a lot
of gluten, we might miss the disease.

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:16.440
Hence, rather than being on
a gluten-free diet,

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:20.820
we want celiac suspects to be
on a gluten-loaded diet.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:25.260
We’re talkin’ 4-6 slices of gluten packed bread
a day, every day for at least a month,

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:28.020
so we can definitively
diagnose the disease.

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:33.300
Why does it matter so much to get a formal
diagnosis if you’re already on a gluten-free diet?

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:37.680
Well, it’s a genetic disease so
you’ll know to test the family,

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:44.940
but most importantly many people on gluten-free
diets are not actually on gluten-free diets.

00:15:44.940 --> 00:15:49.620
Even 20 parts per million can be
toxic to someone with celiac.

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:55.440
Many on so-called gluten-free diets
inadvertently still eat gluten.

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:58.260
Sometimes there’s contamination
of gluten-free products,

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:04.380
so even foods labeled "gluten-free"
may still not be safe for celiac sufferers.

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:07.920
That’s why we need to know.

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:12.900
The irony, editorialized in
a prominent medical journal,

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:16.080
of many celiac patients not
knowing their diagnosis,

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:19.620
while millions of non-sufferers
banish gluten from their lives,

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:22.738
can be considered a
public health farce.

